      "The common explanation “called out ones” comes from the word's parts (<em>ek</em>, out; <em>kaleō</em>, call). That is not wrong, but usage matters more than parts. The word did not mean people called out of the world to hide; it meant people called out of their homes into public assembly. The church is gathered from the world in order to serve it.",
      "So the church is not a building, a denomination or a weekly event. It is a people in union with Christ, gathered wherever two or three meet in his name (Matt 18:20), and sent into every part of life. Its authority is the authority of its Head, used in his way.",
      "<strong>Counter-reading.</strong> Some scholars warn against making too much of the civic meaning, since the Septuagint background (the congregation of Israel) was probably foremost in Jesus' and Matthew's minds, and since Jesus likely spoke Aramaic, using a word like <em>qahal</em> or <em>edah</em>. That is a fair caution. But the Greek-speaking churches of Paul's world heard the word in their own cities every week, and Paul addresses “the ekklesia of God which is at Corinth” (1 Cor 1:2) as if to say: there is another assembly in your city now, and it answers to a different Lord."
